From 600b615f8e97a9d42bf5242055d74ed97206711a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vortiago <atle@havso.net>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 13:43:57 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(ggml-cpu): fuse gated_delta_net with the state snapshot
 cpy
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The CPU backend was the only one that wrote every recurrent state
snapshot into the gated_delta_net result tail and then copied it into
the recurrent cache with a separate cpy. With MTP (K = 4) that cpy moves
n_written x 3 MiB per linear layer of a qwen4exp model on one or two
threads, because dup_bytes splits the strided copy by n_seqs.

Match gated_delta_net followed, after views only, by the cpy of its
snapshot tail into the [D, n_seqs, n_written] cache view, and let the
kernel write each snapshot straight into its cache slot. The cpy is
skipped. The matcher is a C port of ggml_cuda_try_gdn_cache_fusion
(ggml-cuda.cu), with two more checks: the cpy must carry
GGML_TENSOR_FLAG_COMPUTE, because the CPU graph loop skips a node
without it, and the input state must not overlap the cache view.

The kernel takes the snapshot destination and slot stride as arguments,
so the unfused path keeps its output layout. Fusion stays off under
use_ref and GGML_CPU_DISABLE_FUSION. The output is bit-exact.

test_gated_delta_net_cache_fusion gets a mem_size argument for a slot
stride wider than the cpy, as with --parallel 2, and cases for K = 1,
n_tokens < K, n_seqs = 2 and the qwen4exp shape.

